   "text": {
    "L1": "To compare fractions, compare equal-sized parts. The fair comparison depends on the numerators and denominators.",
    "L2": "First, bust this myth: with the same numerator, a bigger denominator does not mean a bigger fraction.",
    "L3": "Ninths are smaller pieces than fifths, so 2/5 is bigger than 2/9.",
    "L4": "If denominators match, the parts are the same size. More parts means the larger fraction, so 5/7 is greater than 3/7.",
    "L5": "If numerators match, the same number of parts is shaded. The smaller denominator makes bigger parts, so 3/5 is greater than 3/8.",
    "L6": "When both numbers differ, change both fractions to equivalent fractions with a common denominator.",
    "L7": "For 5/6 and 4/5, use 30. They become 25/30 and 24/30, so 5/6 is greater.",
    "L8": "You can also use one half as a benchmark. Now choose the greater fraction: 5/6 or 4/5."
   },
